WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.960
Kia LaBeija: I was inspired by voguing because voguing allows you to be anything and anyone you want to be.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.820
Archie Burnett: Vogue is within the realm of a dance family.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.760
Archie: And that's what it's supposed to be—a dance family.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.960
The name voguing comes from Vogue magazine.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.260
The movements that define the dance are based on model poses from the fashion publication.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.060
Willi Ninja, who's called the godfather of voguing,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.920
also drew inspiration from martial arts, ballet, gymnastics, and even pantomimes.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:41.080
In the critically-acclaimed documentary called Paris is Burning, Ninja defines the dance as an extension of throwing shade.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.220
Instead of fighting, two people would settle their beef on the dance-floor.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.680
So whoever had the best moves, would be throwing the best shade.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.000
During the 1970s, in Harlem, houses were formed within the larger drag ballroom scene.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.600
These house serve as surrogate families, primarily for Black and Latino queer youth.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.420
Each house is led by a mother or father who serves as a guide to the ballroom community.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.620
House of LaBeija was the first to form in the late 70s.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:21.260
Other notable houses include the house of Xtravaganza, Ninja, Pendavis, Corey, the House of Wong, the House of Dupree, and many others in New York and across the US.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:26.980
While many of these names come from the founders, other houses are named after couture designers like Chanel and Saint Laurent.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:32.260
Members come together to walk against one another in various categories at elaborate balls.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.480
Beyond the performance or throwing shade, though, these balls create a safe space for empowerment and belonging.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:48.580
Kia Labeija: Whatever you carry with you, you leave it on that floor. Whether it’s suffering for illness, whether it’s suffering for...

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.300
acceptance.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:55.980
Kia: Whether it's suffering from not having a place to call your home.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.660
Kia: Your house becomes your home.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:04.780
After the AIDS epidemic in the 80s, when a large population of the ballroom community was hit hard,

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:10.680
the legendary balls and the houses that organized them also became a place for activism and awareness.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:19.060
Younger generations of voguers can be seen taking the stage at smaller Kiki Balls hosted by Gay Men’s Health Crisis,

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.880
an organization that advocates for prevention of AIDS.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:29.640
Luna Luis Ortiz: It all started at GMHC when, GMHC was trying to figure out how to get young people to come into

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:36.660
the building. And so, Kiki balls began so we could sort of attract them to come in for services.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.500
These balls serve as an outlet for self expression, activism, and offer a

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.560
resource for gay and trans adolescents at risk of HIV, homelessness, abuse and depression.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.020
Outside the ballroom culture, voguing has long been synonymous with fashion and glamour.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.200
It's often confused with Madonna's co-opted version of the dance form.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.100
But it means much more to the community that made it.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:04.120
Archie: Dance is the one thing that you can control with your body.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.160
Archie: That's something that comes from you.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:10.580
Archie: When you can take anything that comes from you,

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.860
and gives you a certain amount of...

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.980
of being comfortable in your own skin. I think that's great!

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.400
Archie: You know? And that is empowerment.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.620
Kia: When you get up there, when you walk that ball,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.140
and everyone is cheering for you. In that moment, you feel...

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.940
...like everybody sees you.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.140
Kia: It makes you feel like you're not alone.

